  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 345 AM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nToday into Tonight: A weak front is slowly pushing south across \nour region. There could be a repeat performance of yesterday in \nterms of microburst potential for locations mainly south of the \nI-20 corridor. The more confident axis for storm development is \nroughly in the Natchez-Hattiesburg corridor, but there is a threat\nfor storms that exists up to about I-20. SPC's depiction of a \nmarginal severe risk looks reasonable given how hot boundary layer\nconditions will be. Any storms that develop this afternoon\nshouldn't persist all that long into the evening with mostly \nquiet conditions to follow. Otherwise, the weak front will bring a\nbrief spell of relatively drier air over parts of north MS and \nAR, potentially encroaching closer to the I-20 corridor during the\nday. While this could help to limit heat indices the farther \nnorth one goes in our area, it is still expected to be quite hot \nin these areas nevertheless. And further south, the humidity will \nremain; thus, another heat advisory is being maintained across \nmuch of the area.\n\nTuesday through the end of the week: The weak front will wash \nout, with low level moisture recovering again areawide Tuesday \ninto Wednesday. Conditions will continue to be seasonably hot each\nday, and it's possible heat stress potential will be high enough \nfor additional heat advisories in some areas, though forecast heat\nindices are looking more marginal beyond Tuesday than what has \nbeen experienced in recent days. Diurnal convection will remain \npossible, initially focused midweek mainly across south MS and LA,\nbut expanding northward through the end of the week. /DL/EC/",
